{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0f55827ae4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-326","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Cresthill Bridge is in central Canada. It opened in 1983.</li><li>Its main span is 1,001 meters.</li><li>The Sable Bridge is in southern Norway. It opened in 1975.</li><li>Its main span is 987 meters.</li><li>Both are suspension bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two bridges.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Cresthill Bridge is in central Canada, and the Sable Bridge is in southern Norway.","The main span of the Cresthill Bridge (1,001 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Bridge (987 meters).","The Cresthill Bridge opened in 1983, while the Sable Bridge opened in 1975.","The Cresthill Bridge, which is in central Canada, is one of several suspension bridges."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3aed79db6e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-332","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Ironwood Bridge is in central Canada. It opened in 1927.</li><li>Its main span is 1,390 meters.</li><li>The Wexford Bridge is in western Scotland. It opened in 1990.</li><li>Its main span is 873 meters.</li><li>Both are cable-stayed bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Ironwood Bridge and the Wexford Bridge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Ironwood Bridge opened in 1927, while the Wexford Bridge opened in 1990.","The main span of the Ironwood Bridge (1,390 meters) is greater than that of the Wexford Bridge (873 meters).","The Ironwood Bridge is in central Canada, and the Wexford Bridge is in western Scotland.","Both the Ironwood Bridge and the Wexford Bridge are cable-stayed bridges."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.143992911c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-333","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Valewood is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1895.</li><li>Its height is 4,187 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in northern Chile. It was first climbed in 1987.</li><li>Its height is 3,886 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Valewood (4,187 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (3,886 meters).","Mount Valewood is in northern Japan, and Mount Glenrock is in northern Chile.","Both Mount Valewood and Mount Glenrock are peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Valewood was first climbed in 1895, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 1987."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e05007448d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-337","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Yarrow Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1893.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 11 meters.</li><li>The Fenwick Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 1902.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Yarrow Observatory and the Fenwick Observatory are radio observatories.","The Yarrow Observatory began operating in 1893, while the Fenwick Observatory began operating in 1902.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Yarrow Observatory (11 meters) is greater than that of the Fenwick Observatory (8 meters).","The Yarrow Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Fenwick Observatory is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2b4a4be4aa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-369","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Valewood National Park is in eastern Poland. It was established in 1978.</li><li>Its area is 8,644 square kilometers.</li><li>Thornbury National Park is in southern New Zealand. It was established in 1894.</li><li>Its area is 5,011 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Valewood National Park and Thornbury National Park.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The area of Valewood National Park (8,644 square kilometers) is greater than that of Thornbury National Park (5,011 square kilometers).","Valewood National Park was established in 1978, while Thornbury National Park was established in 1894.","Valewood National Park is in eastern Poland, and Thornbury National Park is in southern New Zealand.","Both Valewood National Park and Thornbury National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.df91f412be","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-372","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Fenwick Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1983.</li><li>Its height is 176 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 1933.</li><li>Its height is 148 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Fenwick Tower, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several observation towers open to the public.","The height of the Fenwick Tower (176 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (148 meters).","The Fenwick Tower was completed in 1983, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 1933.","Both the Fenwick Tower and the Cresthill Tower are observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.edcbd669d1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Tower is in central Canada, and the Northam Tower is in northern India.","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c0bd27c8dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-387","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Alder Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1919.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 652,421 visitors.</li><li>The Quarry Museum is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1964.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 602,601 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two museums opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Alder Museum is in eastern Australia, and the Quarry Museum is in southern New Zealand.","The annual attendance of the Alder Museum (652,421 visitors) is greater than that of the Quarry Museum (602,601 visitors).","The Alder Museum opened in 1919, while the Quarry Museum opened in 1964.","Both the Alder Museum and the Quarry Museum are art museums with free admission."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b9d2b2dcf5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","Lake Sable is in southern Norway, and Lake Valewood is in northern India.","Both Lake Sable and Lake Valewood are reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d17c0bb4ec","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-399","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Northam National Park is in western Scotland. It was established in 2018.</li><li>Its area is 6,246 square kilometers.</li><li>Alder National Park is in northern India. It was established in 2013.</li><li>Its area is 5,765 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Northam National Park was established in 2018, while Alder National Park was established in 2013.","Northam National Park is in western Scotland, and Alder National Park is in northern India.","Both Northam National Park and Alder National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","The area of Northam National Park (6,246 square kilometers) is greater than that of Alder National Park (5,765 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.94c74fbdd4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-9","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Rahman is an ecologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ecologist Sofia Rahman set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","To investigate the question, Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.335a56b428","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-9","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Rahman is an ecologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Because robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier, Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.552d113a11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-14","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Novak is a psychologist.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","To investigate the question, Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Psychologist Elias Novak set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bb0f08b1dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-22","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","To investigate the question, Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.22089149eb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-24","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Nakamura is a materials scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Nakamura coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Nakamura found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Because coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer, Nakamura concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Nakamura concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","To investigate the question, Nakamura coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2d8e148de1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-25","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a materials scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Takeda coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Takeda found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","To investigate the question, Takeda coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Materials scientist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Takeda found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7f5b80c971","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-27","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Moreau is a materials scientist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer, Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","To investigate the question, Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b922761d9d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-34","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is an entomologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Takeda found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Because bees visited the blue feeders most often, Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Takeda concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eb7951d7a7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-40","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Delacroix is a sociologist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Delacroix surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Delacroix found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Delacroix surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.","Delacroix concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","Delacroix found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","Sociologist Imani Delacroix set out to learn whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b6e3c43091","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-43","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Halvorsen is a geologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Because bare slopes lost several times more soil, Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d9a9870f0f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-44","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Takeda is a geologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because bare slopes lost several times more soil, Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1befb3c560","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-49","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Asante is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Asante concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Asante found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","Nutrition researcher Chen Asante set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a7d4c0a36d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-58","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Iyer is a zoologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Zoologist Diego Iyer set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ad00dd1c2f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-71","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Delacroix is a linguist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors, Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","To investigate the question, Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.76daf38186","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-105","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Kowalski is a chemist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Chemist Amara Kowalski set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5fdeac4c34","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-106","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Brennan is a chemist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Brennan placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Chemist Yara Brennan set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Brennan found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Brennan concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c04e364939","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-111","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is a primatologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","To investigate the question, Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Primatologist Tomás Takeda set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d5c50ebcac","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-136","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Mbeki is an archaeologist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mbeki found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Mbeki concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Mbeki analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Archaeologist Diego Mbeki set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.73bdb14784","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-149","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Kowalski is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Marine ecologist Sofia Kowalski set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.20114be9d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-164","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Kowalski is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Veterinary scientist Lucía Kowalski set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","To investigate the question, Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.af11c1b5df","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-166","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Iyer is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Iyer played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Iyer found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Iyer concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Veterinary scientist Amara Iyer set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","To investigate the question, Iyer played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Iyer found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Iyer concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.682dc56da1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-169","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Petrov is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Petrov added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Petrov found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Petrov concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Petrov added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Petrov concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","Petrov found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Ayumi Petrov set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."}
